#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "b798cfbb-89ac-5d31-adb5-5c43bd483226" :question/math-academy-id "e-5374" :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "140fc720-22c0-5832-afae-c255144291d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5375" :question/problem "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "4ffaeeac-95a0-598a-9880-166d03f810ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-6779" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087555 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "8bff424d-8f2e-528b-acf0-12c33958a132" :question/math-academy-id "e-6817" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9c61364-89e4-5ec3-a514-1fa886fddcb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=225\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=225\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285397} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca2e245-1032-5752-ade8-a7803af1ea17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285398} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6b8347-427c-5ff8-8fe1-dfdc0044329b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285399} {:answer/id #uuid "f73e5d31-eedb-523d-9bb1-d5f27f8eea2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285400} {:answer/id #uuid "e29315a3-c81d-5a6c-9918-c51edc8afbcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f73e5d31-eedb-523d-9bb1-d5f27f8eea2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285400} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c35d5ed-8908-579f-bd21-599749407541" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "384a4738-eb41-5445-8951-5cd80374c2fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-105915" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $15$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=15.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f658a318-42b0-511b-b456-f8782fa2ff34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285404} {:answer/id #uuid "15dbfcfe-475a-5c2e-918f-6a6f36b3b368" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285405} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d27925-46e3-5fab-81ed-7dd3554301bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285406} {:answer/id #uuid "45f9084b-8eba-58fb-9c77-f376633271d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285407} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4462af-e83e-5b61-be83-021ec8c564ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3d27925-46e3-5fab-81ed-7dd3554301bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285406} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee7cc81e-a46d-5c5c-97bd-25350773fc2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "821d7677-e14f-54cf-a344-d6f2cb89596c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106032" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bcb00fd-f159-5bf4-8043-906d1c3abcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299479} {:answer/id #uuid "50881a7c-5ae8-555d-ba04-7ed120fe5f0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299480} {:answer/id #uuid "18f7dc68-6f40-56b8-b9d5-4c3f01828e79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299481} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9a090d-aaf9-5394-8e9c-0c7633220598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299482} {:answer/id #uuid "df72ae08-5e83-5454-8bd9-640c7c40ef33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f9a090d-aaf9-5394-8e9c-0c7633220598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299482} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac316a2d-1ff1-5d3c-aeb2-22bd7ef57252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c45d1161-cc6e-56e5-89da-543c3e758d02" :question/math-academy-id "q-106051" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-3,2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-3,2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a93ff279-b10b-53ce-97a8-65c746433fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285439} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bf81b9-2223-58e7-b783-70cabb89f670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285440} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb33231-df72-5ca8-8640-950b7f6b722b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285441} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e078bf-e8b9-5d98-a4bb-cdc4277ee5f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285442} {:answer/id #uuid "4920736c-ec67-5e91-ac7d-3d520193c11d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bb33231-df72-5ca8-8640-950b7f6b722b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285441} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b463a27-3a4a-5b59-93d3-df9bc4c770d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b4cc6ec-a5c3-5145-a122-c3b8c6352b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106693" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-3).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6a2a01-bb93-5bca-a7ae-d7ce6bd27b75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299486} {:answer/id #uuid "fc10318e-d2a3-56ed-aeab-ff09360174b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299487} {:answer/id #uuid "bc51c0ce-5dfa-5202-b780-9e61826a191e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299488} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e2bbaf-d7a6-5d00-a992-9aec726e8547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299489} {:answer/id #uuid "c16ea69c-7a27-5c2b-887b-d26306361e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0e2bbaf-d7a6-5d00-a992-9aec726e8547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299489} :answer-field/id #uuid "687d1247-73e3-5a10-87fb-3cb9095cb3f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92c2868f-7499-5783-bdd8-0dc234cb675c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106696" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{25}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299491 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abb3ba92-5e0d-5204-a3e0-6ee9079fa1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299493} {:answer/id #uuid "7d174746-1906-5cb2-bcc2-df3c3de0d5b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299494} {:answer/id #uuid "4d50a8d7-79de-5a02-b882-6e32e813ec13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299495} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2851df-526e-537b-8bd7-e5e7ad7d157b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299496} {:answer/id #uuid "c777c3f2-4e38-54c3-a7f1-0b4bd008fed3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b2851df-526e-537b-8bd7-e5e7ad7d157b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299496} :answer-field/id #uuid "5376308a-a84d-5a9f-94db-1ec29dc13d74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4492fb2-623c-509b-a905-5a08f6e893d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-15793" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4(1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d62cf37c-d928-5471-a730-29da52a950e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d62cf37c-d928-5471-a730-29da52a950e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\cos(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299500} :answer-field/id #uuid "74e501c9-f1e9-510e-8202-362c8e8398e7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299499} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4412556e-6fb8-579f-ae78-4929da26e1aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4412556e-6fb8-579f-ae78-4929da26e1aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sin(\\theta)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299502} :answer-field/id #uuid "54e65f65-c0b2-5b94-8cf9-7800c70d0e79" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d1d3adf2-769c-543a-8d79-ad858b0c058d" :question/math-academy-id "q-321891" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/da3a33a3ade10663064516bac22c07459ccf7ce556753ba94d70ee832e947458.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $10$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(10,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r=10.$ Substituting $r=10$ gives\n\n$$\nx=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eaaabe4b-630b-5cf4-b94b-265158077688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaaabe4b-630b-5cf4-b94b-265158077688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299505} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e29c233-ffb8-54e7-aaa0-7c423e7a89d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299504} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67fab322-e6f6-592d-924d-ebe7be333936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67fab322-e6f6-592d-924d-ebe7be333936" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299507} :answer-field/id #uuid "35443237-cdfc-5e40-a53d-b48d7ae809aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf86fc72-2a3b-5b81-8f85-40f278af2f6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-321947" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/11f1e6651d86e0b00884091338029395cd6d2ff44f25b97d010ec750d13bef59.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/2a281b909e4a3577ea3774e9d75c43d97e4d4a69d0f673eed4189834e1ed1c43.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd8ff7a2-0f61-525d-a304-ad1c14eb6d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285472} {:answer/id #uuid "9601c170-7b2e-5c66-9342-9a6a3b9dfb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9601c170-7b2e-5c66-9342-9a6a3b9dfb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285473} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2fedb1f-671d-57c8-aadc-46276312262d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "601ca41a-42dc-5d5a-9575-3ff7aa048d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "601ca41a-42dc-5d5a-9575-3ff7aa048d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285475} :answer-field/id #uuid "f063c384-0a90-5a58-b263-bace855ed6ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f6fa581-0ad2-5257-8314-2e5550da3def" :question/math-academy-id "q-321948" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/ed6f528f7e2b873812d492cbeec386ab56af626a964e98b2929824ec9075d320.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/682f73ccec945de2789ecca71aa6dae650c485189c60ffc64ab7a7aa108665da.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1700e311-fdbb-5249-8ed2-be0517c8158f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1700e311-fdbb-5249-8ed2-be0517c8158f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299510} :answer-field/id #uuid "5944889b-3977-56df-bc2a-70ba1591264d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299509} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b95a4f0b-1788-547f-b86e-4fdec25486f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b95a4f0b-1788-547f-b86e-4fdec25486f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299512} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac2ff9e7-3827-59c8-887f-eab8e33ecc23" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299511} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66900aa4-6091-5a03-a8d4-8375dab94c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299514}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66900aa4-6091-5a03-a8d4-8375dab94c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299514} :answer-field/id #uuid "095831ff-75ba-502f-a387-4d813d1ba657" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ee65cb6-ef5d-5634-9e99-c2bd8cbc9a51" :question/math-academy-id "q-321986" :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+3}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=25.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299515 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169ae951-0e35-5e42-b0b5-af938138de28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299517} {:answer/id #uuid "11715e6a-725a-555b-9d81-46b38d351c2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299518} {:answer/id #uuid "20dd93b1-fc1f-5126-b75e-79f1f9fd8059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299519} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bdcc33-409b-515b-981e-795441cf59a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299520} {:answer/id #uuid "e89b1e9e-f564-5ada-ad82-0643cffc5aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e89b1e9e-f564-5ada-ad82-0643cffc5aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299521} :answer-field/id #uuid "9759745f-9189-5f0b-9b1d-835ddf815557" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "adc81ace-55ff-5a6f-a052-59cb6a0b2712" :question/math-academy-id "q-6877" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e49231b9-c4e0-5e40-823c-15467e4faeca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299524} {:answer/id #uuid "bde30ac3-bb77-5c34-8fe6-6993ecb49f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299525} {:answer/id #uuid "8c3f6eb7-5c13-578e-96e1-27c8fa659ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299526} {:answer/id #uuid "3c11d405-7806-5fbd-98ee-eb3c2e88be62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=24\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299527} {:answer/id #uuid "1415a8f1-3d4d-5653-8642-436000fb5d5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e49231b9-c4e0-5e40-823c-15467e4faeca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299524} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc9a7d12-c15a-5660-a43f-1a1b1b93717f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68b3cf46-70bc-587e-8a8a-2aa59902205d" :question/math-academy-id "q-6893" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/0d90adc7e7badb9c43763febb960b1a8a2b13884c4ce913110264d5c1998ac7b.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $12$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(12,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=12.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0990ea90-4f2d-5bb8-9521-dfe1e2866952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299531} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f1927e-06fc-56f3-ad50-be46c0663d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299532} {:answer/id #uuid "96877dde-7da2-516b-b320-3bfbb7e717e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299533} {:answer/id #uuid "0a65550c-161e-56b9-8f2b-a3dcfeefb534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299534} {:answer/id #uuid "08ebfcd7-b33c-534c-b879-9c8fd10b873a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5f1927e-06fc-56f3-ad50-be46c0663d0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299532} :answer-field/id #uuid "68f73aff-cc65-5348-81a5-79feb3d9ad02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0274a00-5f57-5e2c-8986-f19723d88c89" :question/math-academy-id "q-79161" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(1,6)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(1,6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +6,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299536 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea926645-60a4-5152-bf9c-a62be6a71557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +6,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299538} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5ff9b6-3784-5e77-821c-de7d598b9194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299539} {:answer/id #uuid "352aad4e-1cf5-5f1a-b18a-2c7cbb825fd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +6,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299540} {:answer/id #uuid "2793ba80-2aef-597e-84e9-e9b5672c014d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299541} {:answer/id #uuid "09f54a8e-bb85-51fa-9ab0-b01121d9fa8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -6,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f5ff9b6-3784-5e77-821c-de7d598b9194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299539} :answer-field/id #uuid "91b9079c-659d-54ab-bcd0-d85cf126fd5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "744f03c7-284f-539e-97f7-cf9b288295d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79164" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(4,-6)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(4,-6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -6,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2a75d7f-a83a-53d2-91cf-19783575a28b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299545} {:answer/id #uuid "f25996bf-fd27-5e29-89e3-7d488619155e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299546} {:answer/id #uuid "22c3653e-9278-5059-86c9-30d610217067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299547} {:answer/id #uuid "fc478a22-4762-53c5-90b4-bcbae6233313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299548} {:answer/id #uuid "e655fb51-22af-560b-911f-fc4ad0e14612" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc478a22-4762-53c5-90b4-bcbae6233313" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299548} :answer-field/id #uuid "978b3bf0-a398-5c0c-a0df-b249d600a836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299544}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9a0aa32-a01e-55d8-a12f-e34bc87e72ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-79166" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/5ac5a6ba5e7c041adfe2a36f9e7d835e8090a296cb72adffa5123f67cbed8aa3.png)\n\nThe three-quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a three-quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/6f27674dd7382f9040cdeb8e85ea35ff63e581ef5752b373aa0ab87a0b3e2f0f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required three-quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the three-quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65dbc84a-947b-5650-8306-f0dd3bd145cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299552} {:answer/id #uuid "32676c82-5ca7-56cc-94f5-d84d3d35c7bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299553} {:answer/id #uuid "a678dc53-3d82-5faf-a5ce-08ade66383a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299554} {:answer/id #uuid "16d4ea9e-0021-5012-8ae3-12b0428a6559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299555} {:answer/id #uuid "f7336668-a6bc-5de5-8669-8be21fc89fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299556}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65dbc84a-947b-5650-8306-f0dd3bd145cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299552} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0f3f8b1-a855-5cac-8697-2e89fcb5b44f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97130ad5-e5a5-5a74-b63b-c6293a7f474e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79168" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/a0ec11769fa20feb317ef502f41e1d31ffe539d4b17a51ad46b8487c17752021.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=2.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/9752e135eee9c4e585a007f84b0b8b8d220d4e2e1f027fc8a69aebf27066940e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40208fa8-350f-5d1a-bfb6-eb79193040b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285506} {:answer/id #uuid "dae8a998-a3e3-5155-9d91-acd1f198e8fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285507} {:answer/id #uuid "67666ee2-7893-5d32-9acd-bee7f015aae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285508} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bda015-db44-5c8f-918e-363ef8e23925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285509} {:answer/id #uuid "0e1ef3a4-aaa8-5bf5-b55f-bc2cf177e401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e1ef3a4-aaa8-5bf5-b55f-bc2cf177e401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285510} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b07eb1b-4626-5bc8-ab3d-16bab8d6e553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e74c508-de38-5c1f-966f-ae4879cf576c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79192" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/6b77b1b8a61b5ec7306fbc0b3d70618e7fdce2237bdcbfd017c23c01b80f2979.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-4,5)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-4,5).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb737d9-6de8-5e97-8000-4ef442ee10f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285520} {:answer/id #uuid "26a22685-aa53-52bf-9042-affb9261b909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285521} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb053a0-af9a-58ad-953f-75f723563c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285522} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a500cb-13e8-5c35-94fb-220d40d5cd03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285523} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b156e2-d677-5816-8578-ade01ccf161a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb737d9-6de8-5e97-8000-4ef442ee10f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285520} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f557df-3792-5d35-9fc6-05c142f12f3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "480062d9-9533-5d48-bc7f-73b46822f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79221" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+3}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52698a0c-da5d-5860-9902-5df49ac2439e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299559} {:answer/id #uuid "b5684018-b4fa-5838-a870-3c8045a0b4ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299560} {:answer/id #uuid "0120083a-b2e6-57aa-84f1-e48ee635d4d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299561} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2dff0d-e8b1-55b2-b8c9-9ea04488b826" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299562} {:answer/id #uuid "25fb9489-f74a-51e4-b8fd-4e60877c5716" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0120083a-b2e6-57aa-84f1-e48ee635d4d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299561} :answer-field/id #uuid "16ae94d7-2c1b-5dca-9725-f27bbbe476f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d6f537a3-5f7e-5f24-bef9-d798370c0a41" :question/math-academy-id "q-79222" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-5}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0478b04-03d7-5829-9d4d-2675149472c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299566} {:answer/id #uuid "93a4e948-6c89-5217-ad40-26a1c004aa4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299567} {:answer/id #uuid "62530a29-1774-564f-a02d-e05394fc8d49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299568} {:answer/id #uuid "fe492f17-f98a-58e9-a0f3-1bf974a30c09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299569} {:answer/id #uuid "1e8903b3-20f7-5a33-88ad-63306bbb66a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0478b04-03d7-5829-9d4d-2675149472c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299566} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbcfb8d8-8f15-5e60-b657-5377191adc26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb927d29-41e2-540b-8f69-95f193bb1d7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-79272" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/15dd47ff2ba433da8ad8f6ed25bfa984806e84e4150c0a56697526116cc41431.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=1.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/ddd66398b45c22772af257d6856454b63fde930253090329f99383e4e063fff4.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}]}
